/*
===========================================================================
  cl_netmon.qc -- the lobby network monitor.  Patch 270, Stage 1b.

  WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT HAD TO EXIST.

  The whole public-lobby design turns on one number.  sv_lobby.qc argues that
  an avatar which is a separate non-client entity costs lobby_av_rate updates
  per second, where giving the PLAYER edict a model would cost 66.7 -- because
  SVFTE_DeltaCalcBits (sv_ents.c:998-1006) force-resends origin and both angle
  words every snapshot for any edict with a non-zero pmovetype, delta
  compression deliberately bypassed, and every FTESurf client has one.  That is
  a 4.5x bandwidth difference which is completely invisible on a LAN with two
  clients and which decides whether 32 players is 8 KB/s or 40 KB/s each.

  The first version of this measurement was `cl_shownet 3`, which prints one
  console line per entity update (cl_ents.c:640).  It is correct and it is
  unreadable -- at 66 Hz it buries the console, and counting lines by eye is
  not a measurement.  This panel is the same number, sampled properly.

  THREE THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE THEY WOULD WORK AND DO NOT.  Recorded because
  each cost real time and each would have produced a confident wrong answer.

  1. deltalisten() (#371).  Reads exactly like the right builtin: register a
     callback per model name, count the calls.  But CSQC_DeltaUpdate is
     dispatched from CL_LinkPacketEntities (cl_ents.c:5352), which is a walk
     over cl.currentpackentities once per RENDERED FRAME -- and its input
     contains entities copied forward unchanged (cl_ents.c:1234-1246), so
     unchanged entities are dispatched too.  Counting callbacks measures
     framerate times entity count.  At 500 fps and one avatar it would have
     read ~500 "updates/sec" and looked like a catastrophic netcode bug.

  2. getentity() (#504).  Exposes no sequence and no update marker: the GE_
     list ends at GE_TRAILEFFECTNUM and the default case prints "field is not
     supported".  GE_ACTIVE means "present in the current snapshot", not
     "updated in it" (cl_ents.c:4891-4895), so it is true every frame for a
     stationary avatar.  It IS the right tool for counting bodies, which is
     what it is used for below, and it is the wrong tool for counting updates.

  3. Counting on the SERVER how often Lobby_AvatarPose runs.  This is the
     tempting one because it needs no engine change at all.  It measures how
     often we MOVE the avatar, which is a thing we already know because we
     wrote the loop -- it cannot see delta suppression, PVS, choke or packet
     loss, and it would have reported a perfect 15.0 no matter what arrived.
     The question is what the CLIENT received; only the client can answer it.

  So the count comes from the engine, from the one function that runs exactly
  once per received delta: CLFTE_ReadDelta.  getdeltacount is a ~10-line
  addition (cl_ents.c + pr_csqc.c, Patch 270) returning a free-running
  per-modelindex total, which this file samples twice a second apart.  A
  free-running counter is the shape that cannot be fooled by the sampler's own
  framerate; a callback can be.

  NAMING: everything here is nm_ / NM_, grepped against the whole tree before
  the file existed.  QuakeC has no file scope and fteqcc merges same-named
  globals across files silently -- see the essay at the top of sv_lobby.qc for
  the feature this project has already lost to exactly that.
===========================================================================
*/

/*
  float(float modelindex) getdeltacount

  Declared here rather than in defs/cl_defs.qc on purpose.  That file is
  machine-generated by pr_dumpplatform and gets regenerated; a hand-added line
  in it is a line that quietly disappears one day.  A #0: builtin is resolved
  by NAME at load time (PR_MenuFindBuiltin / the CSQC equivalent), so declaring
  it locally is exactly as valid and cannot be clobbered.
*/
float(float modelindex) getdeltacount = #0:getdeltacount;

// Cvars, cached once per frame.
float  nm_cv_on;
float  nm_cv_x, nm_cv_y;

/*
  The two avatar model indices, resolved per map.

  Per MAP, not once: a modelindex is an index into that map's precache list and
  means nothing on the next one.  CL_ClearState zeroes the engine counters on
  every map change for the same reason, so a stale index here would read a rate
  that never happened.

  BOTH are needed and this is not belt-and-braces.  Lobby_AvatarPose swaps the
  model on every duck and unduck (sv_lobby.qc), so a monitor watching only
  models/player.iqm reads zero for a crouching player -- which looks exactly
  like "the avatar stopped updating".
*/
float  nm_mi_stand;
float  nm_mi_duck;

// The one-second window.  Same shape as ui_hl_* in cl_hud.qc:210-213/332-342:
// a count and a window, not a running average, because the answer wanted is
// "about 15" or "about 67" and either is legible to one decimal.
float  nm_c_last;               // counter total when the window opened
float  nm_t_last;               // cltime the window opened
float  nm_rate;                 // last full window, deltas/sec, all bodies
float  nm_bodies;               // bodies counted at the last window close
float  nm_have;                 // a full window has closed at least once

/*
  Does this engine have getdeltacount?

  Not optional politeness.  A #0: builtin the engine does not know resolves to
  PF_Fixme, which errors when CALLED rather than at load -- and NM_Frame calls
  it every frame from CSQC_UpdateView.  So a csprogs.dat from this build run
  against an ftesurf64.exe from before Patch 270 would not degrade to a missing
  panel, it would take the whole client down on the first frame of every map.
  That pairing is not hypothetical: the progs and the exe are separate files and
  people copy one without the other.

  checkbuiltin (cl_defs.qc:1095) answers it once, at CSQC_Init.
*/
float  nm_ok;

void() NM_RegisterCvars =
{
	nm_ok = checkbuiltin(getdeltacount);

	// Default 0.  This is a diagnostic, not a HUD panel, and the house rule is
	// that a solo session is byte-identical to before the feature existed.
	registercvar("hud_net",   "0", CVAR_ARCHIVE);
	registercvar("hud_net_x", "0.78", CVAR_ARCHIVE);
	registercvar("hud_net_y", "0.30", CVAR_ARCHIVE);
};

/*
  Resolve the avatar model indices for this map.

  Linear scan of modelnameforindex (#334) rather than anything cleverer: the
  precache list is short, this runs once per map, and there is no builtin that
  goes the other way.  The list is terminated by the first empty name, which is
  the same termination PF_DeltaListen uses (pr_csqc.c:5944-5947).
*/
void() NM_WorldLoaded =
{
	local float i;
	local string s;

	nm_mi_stand = 0;
	nm_mi_duck  = 0;

	nm_c_last = 0;
	nm_t_last = 0;
	nm_rate   = 0;
	nm_bodies = 0;
	nm_have   = FALSE;

	for (i = 1; i < 4096; i = i + 1)
	{
		s = modelnameforindex(i);
		if (s == "" || !s)
			break;
		if (s == "models/player.iqm")
			nm_mi_stand = i;
		else if (s == "models/player_duck.iqm")
			nm_mi_duck = i;
	}
};

/*
  How many lobby avatars are on screen.

  GE_ACTIVE here means exactly what is wanted -- "the client currently knows
  about this entity" -- and the count is the divisor that turns a per-MODEL
  delta count into a per-BODY rate.  Without it the panel reads 225 Hz for
  fifteen players at 15 Hz and looks like the bug it exists to rule out.

  Your OWN avatar is deliberately not counted, and it cannot be: the server
  never sends it to you (.nodrawtoclient, see Lobby_AvatarSelf), so it is
  absent from both this scan and the delta counter.  Numerator and denominator
  therefore agree without special-casing -- unless lobby_av_self is 1, in which
  case both include it and they still agree.
*/
float() NM_CountBodies =
{
	local float i, n, mi, c, act;

	if (!nm_mi_stand && !nm_mi_duck)
		return 0;

	n = getentity(0, GE_MAXENTS);
	c = 0;
	for (i = 1; i < n; i = i + 1)
	{
		// Through a float local: getentity is declared __variant (cl_defs.qc:1976)
		// and fteqcc rejects `!` applied to one directly.
		act = getentity(i, GE_ACTIVE);
		if (!act)
			continue;
		mi = getentity(i, GE_MODELINDEX);
		if ((nm_mi_stand && mi == nm_mi_stand) || (nm_mi_duck && mi == nm_mi_duck))
			c = c + 1;
	}
	return c;
};

/*
  Sampled every frame, drawn only when hud_net is on, and that split is
  deliberate.  The engine counter is FREE-RUNNING: if the window only advanced
  while the panel was visible, the first reading after switching hud_net on
  would divide every delta received while it was off by one second, and print a
  number in the hundreds.  That is exactly the sort of confident wrong answer
  this file exists to stop producing, so the window runs regardless and only
  the body scan and the drawing are gated.
*/
void() NM_Frame =
{
	local float now, tot, dt;

	nm_cv_on = cvar("hud_net");
	nm_cv_x  = cvar("hud_net_x");
	nm_cv_y  = cvar("hud_net_y");

	if (!nm_ok)
		return;

	tot = getdeltacount(nm_mi_stand) + getdeltacount(nm_mi_duck);
	now = cltime;

	/*
	  Window restart, and the second half of the test is not paranoia.
	  cltime running BACKWARDS is a map change or a demo seek, and `tot`
	  dropping is the engine's counter having been zeroed under us by
	  CL_ClearState -- either one would otherwise produce a single enormous
	  negative or positive spike that reads as a real measurement.
	*/
	if (!nm_t_last || now < nm_t_last || tot < nm_c_last)
	{
		nm_t_last = now;
		nm_c_last = tot;
		return;
	}

	dt = now - nm_t_last;
	if (dt < 1)
		return;

	nm_rate = (tot - nm_c_last) / dt;

	// Only scanned when the panel is up: this is the one loop here that is not
	// O(1), and nothing else reads nm_bodies.
	if (nm_cv_on)
		nm_bodies = NM_CountBodies();

	nm_have = TRUE;

	nm_t_last = now;
	nm_c_last = tot;
};

void(vector scr) NM_Draw =
{
	local vector p, col;
	local float sz, lh, rx, per;

	if (!nm_cv_on)
		return;

	sz = 8 * ui_cv_scale;
	lh = sz + 2 * ui_cv_scale;
	p  = [scr_x * nm_cv_x, scr_y * nm_cv_y, 0];
	rx = p_x + 104 * ui_cv_scale;       // the column the numbers END on

	Font_Set(ui_font_ui);

	HUD_Text(p, "net", sz, '0.6 0.8 1.0', 1);                          p_y += lh;

	if (!nm_ok)
	{
		// The engine predates Patch 270.  Named rather than blank, because a
		// blank panel reads as "zero updates" -- the failure it is here to find.
		HUD_Text(p, "engine too old", sz, '1.0 0.4 0.4', 1);
		return;
	}

	if (!nm_mi_stand && !nm_mi_duck)
	{
		// Said out loud rather than drawn as 0.0.  No avatar model precached on
		// this map means either the server is not in lobby mode or Lobby_Precache
		// did not run -- both are real answers and neither is "0 updates".
		HUD_Text(p, "no avatar models", sz, '0.9 0.7 0.4', 1);
		return;
	}

	if (!nm_have)
	{
		HUD_Text(p, "sampling...", sz, '0.7 0.7 0.7', 1);
		return;
	}

	HUD_Text (p, "bodies", sz, '0.7 0.7 0.7', 1);
	HUD_TextR([rx, p_y, 0], ftos(nm_bodies), sz, '1 1 1', 1);          p_y += lh;

	HUD_Text (p, "upd/s", sz, '0.7 0.7 0.7', 1);
	HUD_TextR([rx, p_y, 0], sprintf("%.1f", nm_rate), sz, '1 1 1', 1); p_y += lh;

	/*
	  The row the whole panel exists for.

	  Colour is a verdict, and the thresholds are the two outcomes the design
	  predicts rather than round numbers.  Anything near 66.7 means the pose is
	  being sent every server frame -- which is what happens if the avatar ever
	  becomes a client edict and SVFTE_DeltaCalcBits' force-resend branch starts
	  firing on it.  That is the failure this measurement is for, so it is red.
	*/
	if (nm_bodies <= 0)
	{
		HUD_Text (p, "per body", sz, '0.7 0.7 0.7', 1);
		HUD_TextR([rx, p_y, 0], "-", sz, '0.6 0.6 0.6', 1);
	}
	else
	{
		per = nm_rate / nm_bodies;
		if (per > 45)                                 col = '1.0 0.4 0.4';
		else if (per > 30)                            col = '1.0 0.8 0.4';
		else                                          col = '0.5 0.9 0.5';

		HUD_Text (p, "per body", sz, '0.7 0.7 0.7', 1);
		HUD_TextR([rx, p_y, 0], sprintf("%.1f", per), sz, col, 1);
	}
};
